---
import { MAL_ENTENDIDOS } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import { ProvidersLab } from "../../components/ProvidersLab.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/ProvidersLab.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
import "@ovh/depth-ui/src/stage.css";
import "../../components/Explorer.css";
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="The three providers — OpenLabs"
  description="You called tracer.startSpan. Who decides whether it ever leaves the process? The resource, the sampler, the processors, the limits and the flush — and the OTLP envelope is the same tree seen from the other side."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>You called <code>startSpan</code>. Who decides whether it leaves?</h1>
        <p>
          Not the tracer. A <code>Tracer</code> has no configuration at all — it
          holds an instrumentation scope and nothing else. Everything that
          decides the fate of a span is on the <b>provider</b>: the resource
          stamped on it, the sampler consulted before it exists, the processors
          it passes through, the limits it is trimmed to, and the moment
          somebody calls flush.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a second thing this lab shows, and it is the reason it is the
          first one in this handbook. Open the envelope on the right while the
          simulation runs: <code>ResourceSpans → ScopeSpans → Span</code> is,
          field for field, <b>provider → tracer → span</b>. The OTLP payload is
          not a serialization of the SDK — it <em>is</em> the SDK&rsquo;s object
          graph, written down. That is why <code>service.name</code> sits one
          layer above the spans, and why nobody can put it on a span.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>Three providers, one process.</p>
        <p>One tick is one second, so the spec&rsquo;s defaults fit unchanged.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-stage">
      <ProvidersLab client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <p>
          Four perturbations, and none of them is scripted — each one is the same
          run with one control moved.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Drag the sampling ratio down and tick <em>RECORD_ONLY</em>.</b> The
            middle outlet lights up. Those spans are created, recorded and
            readable inside the process, and the exporter never sees one of them.
            Almost nobody knows that outlet exists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Set <code>maxQueueSize</code> to 4 and let it run.</b> The queue
            refuses. The drop is a fact in the model, not a silence — it leaves
            by its own outlet and it is counted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Kill the process, then kill it again with <code>ForceFlush</code>.</b>
            Same run, one parameter. Without the flush the queue dies with the
            process; with it, one control line descends to every registered
            processor and the batch leaves first. That is why spans vanish at the
            end of a short job.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Turn on <code>trace_based</code>, then drop sampling to zero.</b>
            Logs stop arriving. The only thing joining those two facts is a single
            dashed line crossing from one provider into another.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>
          Answer first, then run it. Predicting and being wrong is what makes the
          answer stick; watching and recognising is not the same thing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="A sampler returns RECORD_ONLY for a span. What reaches the exporter?"
          opcoes={[
            "Nothing — the processor is called, the exporter is not",
            "The span, without attributes",
            "Nothing — the processor is never called either",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="The spec's reaction table is explicit: with RECORD_ONLY the SpanProcessor's OnStart and OnEnd are invoked, and the span is not exported. It exists, it is recorded, it is readable in-process, and it never leaves — which is exactly why 'dropped' and 'not sampled' are not the same thing."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Every row below is a belief worth naming, because the drawing above is
          arranged to take it apart. The right column is the spec, with the link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the spec says</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {MAL_ENTENDIDOS.map((m) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                <td>
                  {m.spec}{" "}
                  <a href={m.fonte} rel="noopener">spec&nbsp;→</a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What is deliberately not here</h2>
        <p>
          The channel out of the process is <b>opaque</b>, and it says so when you
          click it. HTTP/2 framing, length prefixes and stream ids are real, and
          they answer a different question than this lab asks — they are the
          <i>gRPC over HTTP/2</i> annex and the Collector pipeline lab. What
          happens <em>inside</em> the Collector is not modelled here either: it is
          another program, it is drawn outside the process for that reason, and
          drawing it as an empty box would be worse than saying so.
        </p>
        <p>
          Retry is missing on purpose too, and its absence is content: the spec
          says the default SDK must <b>not</b> implement retry, because the logic
          depends on the protocol. Modelling it here would be a lie about the SDK.
        </p>

        <h2>Check yourself</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Two <code>TracerProvider</code>s in one process, with different
            resources. What fails? <b>Nothing.</b> You get two
            <code>resourceSpans</code> with different <code>service.name</code>,
            no error anywhere, and a backend that quietly stops correlating them.
          </li>
          <li>
            The queue is full and the metric store is at its cardinality limit.
            Do they lose data the same way? <b>No.</b> The queue refuses and the
            span is gone; the store collapses the excess into a single
            <code>otel.metric.overflow</code> row and the sum is preserved. Same
            problem — finite memory — two different lies.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your SDK never came up. What is the first symptom? <b>Silence.</b> No
            exception, no warning, a no-op provider swallowing spans. It is the
            most common first-instrumentation failure, and the only way to teach
            it is to put a number on it.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  /*
    O lab é a peça mais larga da página de propósito: o texto do desenho é
    medido em unidades da vista, então cada pixel de largura que o palco ganha
    vira texto legível. O limite antigo, de 96rem, deixava mil pixels de tela
    sobrando num monitor grande enquanto o desenho ficava espremido.
  */
  .lab-stage {
    max-width: 118rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
  }

  .lab-prosa h2 {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-1);
    font-size: var(--size-step-1);
  }

  .lab-prosa ol {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .lab-prosa li {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-1);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    align-self: start;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
    line-height: 1.6;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos td:first-child {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
